{
  "result": "Everyone has a junk drawer. Not the silverware drawer with its polite little dividers, but the real one. The one that sticks if you pull too fast, that rattles.\n\nInside: dead batteries, a tape measure, three Allen keys from furniture that no longer exists, a single birthday candle, twist ties, a novelty fridge magnet from a trip you barely remember, a pair of scissors that are not quite sharp enough, and somewhere at the bottom, a key. You have no idea what it opens. You will never throw it away.\n\nI love this drawer because it's an honest autobiography. We like to think we curate our lives, but mostly we accumulate. The shelf with the travel books, the gallery wall, that's the edited press release. The junk drawer is the first draft, full of annotations.\n\nAnthropologists dig through middens to learn about ancient people, looking at what they threw away. If someone excavated my junk drawer in two thousand years, they'd conclude I was a ritualistic people who worshipped small hexagons and hoarded copper energy cylinders.\n\nThere is comfort in keeping things just in case. It is a small rebellion against impermanence. The world wants you to be streamlined, optimized, minimal. The drawer says no. It says: what if we need this? What if this useless thing becomes useful again?\n\nEventually you move, you dump the drawer into a box, sort it for ten minutes, then pour it into a new drawer in a new kitchen. The key comes with you.",
